{"module":"borders","voice":"male","duration":219.9,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Borders: who gets to be American","start":0.0,"end":2.64,"words":[[0.06,0.58],[0.89,1.06],[1.06,1.41],[1.41,1.58],[1.58,1.76],[1.76,2.45]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Immigration law has always been a way of deciding who counts as American.","start":3.44,"end":8.72,"words":[[3.48,4.26],[4.26,4.42],[4.42,4.66],[4.66,5.13],[5.13,5.37],[5.37,5.53],[5.53,5.76],[5.76,5.92],[5.92,6.55],[6.55,6.71],[6.71,7.18],[7.18,7.34],[7.34,8.05]]},{"el":"intro","text":"For most of U.S. history, it did so openly by race.","start":9.07,"end":13.0,"words":[[9.11,9.23],[9.23,9.55],[9.55,9.68],[9.68,10.0],[10.31,10.82],[11.04,11.17],[11.17,11.36],[11.36,11.55],[11.55,11.99],[11.99,12.19],[12.19,12.5]]},{"el":"h0","text":"“Free white persons”","start":13.35,"end":15.47,"words":[[13.36,13.8],[13.8,14.38],[14.38,15.25]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The first Naturalization Act (1790) limited citizenship by naturalization to “free white persons.”","start":16.17,"end":23.66,"words":[[16.22,16.36],[16.36,16.65],[16.65,17.82],[17.82,18.03],[18.03,19.05],[19.31,19.82],[19.82,20.54],[20.54,20.76],[20.76,21.92],[21.92,22.07],[22.07,22.29],[22.29,22.58],[22.58,23.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Courts then had to decide who was white.","start":24.01,"end":26.45,"words":[[24.05,24.32],[24.32,24.53],[24.53,24.74],[24.74,24.88],[24.88,25.3],[25.3,25.44],[25.44,25.64],[25.64,25.99]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In Ozawa (1922) the Supreme Court ruled a Japanese immigrant was not; in Thind (1923) it ruled that an Indian Sikh immigrant was not either, even though anthropologists classed him as “Caucasian.”","start":26.8,"end":40.37,"words":[[26.85,27.0],[27.0,27.36],[27.36,28.44],[28.69,28.83],[28.83,29.27],[29.27,29.48],[29.48,29.77],[29.77,29.91],[29.91,30.49],[30.49,31.07],[31.07,31.28],[31.28,31.57],[31.82,31.97],[31.97,32.33],[32.33,33.48],[33.73,33.87],[33.87,34.16],[34.16,34.38],[34.38,34.52],[34.52,34.95],[34.95,35.17],[35.17,35.75],[35.75,35.96],[35.96,36.18],[36.18,36.54],[36.79,37.08],[37.08,37.29],[37.29,38.37],[38.37,38.73],[38.73,38.95],[38.95,39.09],[39.09,39.81]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Racial bars to naturalization weren't fully removed until 1952.","start":40.72,"end":46.31,"words":[[40.78,41.26],[41.26,41.5],[41.5,41.66],[41.66,42.96],[42.96,43.28],[43.28,43.61],[43.61,44.09],[44.09,44.5],[44.5,45.71]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Chinese Exclusion","start":47.21,"end":49.08,"words":[[47.25,47.98],[47.98,48.93]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Page Act (1875) effectively barred Chinese women, and the Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) barred Chinese laborers, the first U.S. law to exclude a group by nationality.","start":49.78,"end":62.05,"words":[[49.83,49.97],[49.97,50.32],[50.32,50.53],[50.53,51.66],[51.91,52.54],[52.54,52.75],[52.75,53.24],[53.24,53.67],[53.91,54.12],[54.12,54.26],[54.26,54.76],[54.76,55.39],[55.39,55.6],[55.6,56.37],[56.62,56.83],[56.83,57.32],[57.32,57.96],[58.2,58.34],[58.34,58.63],[58.63,58.98],[59.33,59.47],[59.47,59.61],[59.61,60.1],[60.1,60.24],[60.24,60.53],[60.53,60.74],[60.74,61.51]]},{"el":"s1","text":"It wasn't repealed until 1943.","start":62.4,"end":65.8,"words":[[62.45,62.61],[62.61,63.1],[63.1,63.58],[63.58,63.98],[63.98,65.19]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Chinese immigrants were held and interrogated at Angel Island in San Francisco Bay (1910–1940).","start":66.15,"end":73.58,"words":[[66.22,66.73],[66.73,67.38],[67.38,67.53],[67.53,67.82],[67.82,68.03],[68.03,68.9],[68.9,69.05],[69.05,69.55],[69.55,69.99],[69.99,70.13],[70.13,70.35],[70.35,71.07],[71.07,71.29],[71.29,73.03]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Quotas by race","start":74.48,"end":76.11,"words":[[74.53,75.21],[75.21,75.5],[75.5,75.89]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Immigration Act of 1924 set quotas based on the national origins of the U.S. population in 1890, to favor Northern Europeans, and barred almost all immigration from Asia.","start":76.81,"end":90.38,"words":[[76.86,77.01],[77.01,77.78],[77.78,78.01],[78.01,78.16],[78.16,79.31],[79.31,79.54],[79.54,80.07],[80.07,80.46],[80.46,80.61],[80.61,80.76],[80.76,81.3],[81.3,81.91],[81.91,82.07],[82.07,82.22],[82.22,82.6],[82.98,83.83],[83.83,83.98],[83.98,84.9],[85.17,85.32],[85.32,85.7],[85.7,86.08],[86.08,86.93],[87.19,87.42],[87.42,87.65],[87.65,88.19],[88.19,88.34],[88.34,89.11],[89.11,89.41],[89.41,89.8]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Eugenicists testified in its favor.","start":90.73,"end":94.05,"words":[[90.78,91.8],[91.8,92.56],[92.56,92.73],[92.73,92.98],[92.98,93.49]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The quota system lasted until the Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965.","start":94.4,"end":100.42,"words":[[94.45,94.6],[94.6,95.04],[95.04,95.49],[95.49,95.93],[95.93,96.3],[96.3,96.45],[96.45,97.19],[97.19,97.41],[97.41,98.16],[98.16,98.38],[98.38,98.53],[98.53,99.86]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Mexican Americans: repatriation and “Operation Wetback”","start":101.32,"end":105.57,"words":[[101.37,101.98],[101.98,102.74],[103.01,104.01],[104.01,104.24],[104.24,104.93],[104.93,105.39]]},{"el":"s3","text":"During the Depression (1929–1936), federal, state and local officials pushed out hundreds of thousands of Mexicans and Mexican Americans; estimates range from about 400,000 to more than a million, and many were U.S. citizens, including children.","start":106.27,"end":122.48,"words":[[106.32,106.74],[106.74,106.87],[106.87,107.36],[107.36,109.5],[109.71,110.2],[110.42,110.72],[110.72,110.91],[110.91,111.28],[111.28,111.7],[111.7,111.95],[111.95,112.13],[112.13,112.62],[112.62,112.75],[112.75,113.24],[113.24,113.36],[113.36,113.91],[113.91,114.09],[114.09,114.58],[114.58,115.2],[115.41,115.9],[115.9,116.27],[116.27,116.51],[116.51,116.82],[116.82,117.8],[117.8,117.92],[117.92,118.04],[118.04,118.23],[118.23,118.35],[118.35,118.78],[118.99,119.18],[119.18,119.42],[119.42,119.54],[119.54,119.85],[120.16,120.71],[120.92,121.41],[121.41,121.96]]},{"el":"s3","text":"California apologized in 2005.","start":122.83,"end":126.13,"words":[[122.87,123.55],[123.55,124.38],[124.38,124.53],[124.53,125.58]]},{"el":"s3","text":"During World War II the Bracero program recruited Mexican workers; in 1954 “Operation Wetback” carried out mass deportations.","start":126.48,"end":136.49,"words":[[126.53,127.08],[127.08,127.39],[127.39,127.55],[127.55,128.17],[128.17,128.33],[128.33,129.03],[129.03,129.66],[129.66,130.29],[130.29,130.91],[130.91,131.38],[131.65,131.81],[131.81,132.91],[132.91,133.61],[133.61,134.08],[134.08,134.47],[134.47,134.71],[134.71,134.94],[134.94,135.88]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The government claimed over a million removals that year; historians consider that number inflated.","start":136.84,"end":143.29,"words":[[136.9,137.04],[137.04,137.68],[137.68,138.11],[138.11,138.39],[138.39,138.53],[138.53,138.96],[138.96,139.52],[139.52,139.74],[139.74,140.02],[140.27,140.98],[140.98,141.47],[141.47,141.69],[141.69,142.04],[142.04,142.75]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Japanese American incarceration","start":144.19,"end":146.61,"words":[[144.24,144.87],[144.87,145.49],[145.49,146.42]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Executive Order 9066 (February 1942) led to the forced removal of about 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry from the West Coast, two-thirds of them U.S. citizens, to camps behind barbed wire.","start":147.31,"end":161.43,"words":[[147.36,148.01],[148.01,148.2],[148.2,149.69],[149.69,150.21],[150.21,151.05],[151.28,151.47],[151.47,151.6],[151.6,151.73],[151.73,151.99],[151.99,152.45],[152.45,152.58],[152.58,152.9],[152.9,154.52],[154.52,154.84],[154.84,154.97],[154.97,155.49],[155.49,156.01],[156.01,156.27],[156.27,156.4],[156.4,156.66],[156.66,157.05],[157.27,157.66],[157.66,157.79],[157.79,157.99],[157.99,158.31],[158.63,159.22],[159.44,159.57],[159.57,159.9],[159.9,160.35],[160.35,160.61],[160.61,160.93]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Supreme Court upheld it in Korematsu (1944).","start":161.78,"end":165.71,"words":[[161.83,161.99],[161.99,162.47],[162.47,162.71],[162.71,163.19],[163.19,163.35],[163.35,163.51],[163.51,164.07],[164.07,165.19]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Civil Liberties Act of 1988 apologized and paid $20,000 to each surviving person who had been incarcerated, and in 2018 the Court said Korematsu “was gravely wrong the day it was decided.”","start":166.06,"end":179.82,"words":[[166.11,166.26],[166.26,166.64],[166.64,167.16],[167.16,167.39],[167.39,167.54],[167.54,168.66],[168.66,169.48],[169.48,169.71],[169.71,170.01],[170.01,171.28],[171.28,171.43],[171.43,171.65],[171.65,172.25],[172.25,172.62],[172.62,172.77],[172.77,173.0],[173.0,173.22],[173.22,174.2],[174.46,174.68],[174.68,174.83],[174.83,175.73],[175.73,175.88],[175.88,176.1],[176.1,176.33],[176.33,176.85],[176.85,177.08],[177.08,177.6],[177.6,177.83],[177.83,177.98],[177.98,178.2],[178.2,178.35],[178.35,178.57],[178.57,179.25]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Today","start":180.72,"end":181.49,"words":[[180.76,181.31]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In 2018 the “zero tolerance” policy separated thousands of children from their parents at the border.","start":182.19,"end":188.49,"words":[[182.24,182.37],[182.37,183.15],[183.15,183.28],[183.28,183.67],[183.67,184.19],[184.19,184.58],[184.58,185.23],[185.23,185.75],[185.75,185.88],[185.88,186.4],[186.4,186.66],[186.66,186.85],[186.85,187.37],[187.37,187.5],[187.5,187.63],[187.63,187.96]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Immigration detention holds tens of thousands of people on a given day.","start":188.84,"end":193.73,"words":[[188.89,189.63],[189.63,190.22],[190.22,190.66],[190.66,190.96],[190.96,191.11],[191.11,191.7],[191.7,191.84],[191.84,192.21],[192.21,192.36],[192.36,192.51],[192.51,192.88],[192.88,193.17]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In September 2025 the Supreme Court allowed immigration agents in the Los Angeles area to resume stops that a lower court had found relied on factors including apparent ethnicity, language and type of work, while the case continues.","start":194.08,"end":210.74,"words":[[194.12,194.28],[194.28,194.93],[194.93,196.22],[196.22,196.38],[196.38,196.87],[196.87,197.11],[197.11,197.51],[197.51,198.32],[198.32,198.97],[198.97,199.13],[199.13,199.29],[199.29,199.53],[199.53,200.18],[200.18,200.58],[200.58,200.74],[200.74,201.23],[201.23,201.63],[201.63,201.88],[201.88,202.04],[202.04,202.36],[202.36,202.6],[202.6,202.85],[202.85,203.25],[203.25,203.73],[203.73,203.9],[203.9,204.38],[204.38,205.03],[205.03,205.59],[205.59,206.32],[206.6,207.25],[207.25,207.49],[207.49,207.81],[207.81,207.98],[207.98,208.3],[208.58,208.91],[208.91,209.07],[209.07,209.39],[209.39,210.2]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A CAREN-style law protects anyone, citizen or not, from being reported to police in order to discriminate.","start":211.09,"end":218.96,"words":[[211.14,211.3],[211.3,212.1],[212.1,212.26],[212.26,212.9],[212.9,213.46],[213.74,214.3],[214.3,214.38],[214.38,214.7],[214.98,215.3],[215.3,215.7],[215.7,216.26],[216.26,216.42],[216.42,216.82],[216.82,216.98],[216.98,217.22],[217.22,217.38],[217.38,218.42]]}]}